  "html": "<h2>Format and Structure </h2>\n<p><span data-contrast=\"auto\">Following a restructuring that was initiated in the 2024/25 season, as set out as part of Malta FA President Bjorn Vassallo’s strategy, the league is now fully autonomous, and the number of clubs has been reduced from 14 to 12.</span><span data-ccp-props=\"{}\"> </span></p>\n<p><span data-contrast=\"auto\">While the Malta FA is still responsible for the licensing and the regulation aspect, the league’s organisation is handled by the Malta Premier League.</span><span data-ccp-props=\"{}\"> </span></p>\n<p><span data-contrast=\"auto\">Further details can be obtained from the </span><a rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://www.maltapremierleague.mt/\" target=\"_blank\"><span data-contrast=\"none\">Malta Premier’s official website</span></a><span data-contrast=\"auto\">.</span><span data-ccp-props=\"{}\"> </span></p>\n<h2>Qualification for European Competitions </h2>\n<p><span data-contrast=\"auto\">The Malta Premier provides direct access to European football.</span><span data-ccp-props=\"{}\"> </span></p>\n<p><span data-contrast=\"auto\">The league champions qualify for the UEFA Champions League qualifying rounds, while other top-placed teams – together with the winner of the Meridianbet FA Trophy – earn entry into the UEFA Conference League qualifying rounds.</span><span data-ccp-props=\"{}\"> </span></p>\n<p><span data-contrast=\"auto\">These opportunities allow Maltese clubs to compete on the international stage, gain valuable experience, and enhance the reputation of Maltese football abroad.</span><span data-ccp-props=\"{}\"> </span></p>",
